["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"Don&#x27;t stand between your kid and the screen — stand beside them The real mechanism of toxic stress is exposure without an adult to buffer it; the more you can be on your kid&#x27;s side, with technology as the shared opponent, the more the relationship itself becomes the strongest protection available in the digital age. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 284: ENCORE: Screens, Toxic Stress &amp; Staying Connected with Dr. Nadine Burke Harris Screen time Toxic stress ACEs Adolescence AI companions Parent-child relationship ACEs / adverse childhood experiences toxic stress response buffering caregiving upstander\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-11-asklisa-别站在孩子和屏幕之间-要站在孩子这边/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/c55e4104-cbbe-11ec-93d2-ab245e93324f/image/478fcfa676f260bc9a5628875ab7e0af.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">47:23</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0811</span></div>\n<h2>Don&#x27;t stand between your kid and the screen — stand beside them</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The real mechanism of toxic stress is exposure without an adult to buffer it; the more you can be on your kid&#x27;s side, with technology as the shared opponent, the more the relationship itself becomes the strongest protection available in the digital age.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Screen time</span><span class=\"tag\">Toxic stress</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"Deep-tech companies die of organizational failure, not technical failure How fast a startup can iterate determines how long it can keep winning — and iteration speed comes from &quot;infrastructure&quot; like procurement, hiring and performance review. Moving approvals up to the budget layer and replacing reviews with eigenreviews is worth more investment than agonizing over any specific technology. Y Combinator Y Combinator Max Hodak: How Startups Build Speed Organizational efficiency Hiring mechanics Performance review Startup fundraising Deep tech Brain-computer interfaces eigenreviews AI-resistant homework vibe coding tropicalization\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-ycsp-深科技公司败给组织系统-而不是技术失败/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_episode/23401959/23401959-1786397693931-160118749202e.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">57:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Y Combinator</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Deep-tech companies die of organizational failure, not technical failure</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">How fast a startup can iterate determines how long it can keep winning — and iteration speed comes from &quot;infrastructure&quot; like procurement, hiring and performance review. Moving approvals up to the budget layer and replacing reviews with eigenreviews is worth more investment than agonizing over any specific technology.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Organizational efficiency</span><span class=\"tag\">Hiring mechanics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Smashing threshing machines bought 252 death sentences, and the workers still lost Nineteenth-century English farm laborers protested unemployment by destroying threshing machines. What they got was 252 death sentences, 481 or 505 people transported to Australia, and the limited parliamentary reform of 1832 — the laborers&#x27; fortunes did not change, but the political map did. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class Swing Riots Agricultural Revolution Technological Unemployment British History Parliamentary Reform Machine-Breaking Poor Laws threshing machine flail Corn Laws workhouse select vestries rotten boroughs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-stuffmissed-砸烂打谷机也没能换来工人好日子/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.omnycontent.com/d/programs/e73c998e-6e60-432f-8610-ae210140c5b1/cfb428ef-eafc-44d0-9d09-ae2701747e6f/image.jpg?t=1749829805&amp;size=Medium\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">36:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Smashing threshing machines bought 252 death sentences, and the workers still lost</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Nineteenth-century English farm laborers protested unemployment by destroying threshing machines. What they got was 252 death sentences, 481 or 505 people transported to Australia, and the limited parliamentary reform of 1832 — the laborers&#x27; fortunes did not change, but the political map did.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Agricultural Revolution</span><span class=\"tag\">Technological Unemployment</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Custom Silicon Isn&#x27;t About Saving Money, It&#x27;s About Escaping Nvidia&#x27;s Generational Grip The real motive behind hyperscaler custom silicon is not cost but control: not being held hostage to Nvidia&#x27;s generational cadence, while using sheer scale to squeeze down networking and compute costs. The Broadcom tax is cheaper than the Nvidia tax, but only the largest players can afford to play. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato What do we actually think about Hyperscale Silicon?? // The AI Hardware Podcast S2E9 Hyperscalers Custom silicon AI hardware Nvidia Arm TPU Nitro RISC-V Arm CSS\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-techtechpotato-超大规模自研芯片-不只为省钱-更怕被-nvidia-绑架/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i3.ytimg.com/vi/6_QXSyIhqB4/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">30:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Custom Silicon Isn&#x27;t About Saving Money, It&#x27;s About Escaping Nvidia&#x27;s Generational Grip</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The real motive behind hyperscaler custom silicon is not cost but control: not being held hostage to Nvidia&#x27;s generational cadence, while using sheer scale to squeeze down networking and compute costs. The Broadcom tax is cheaper than the Nvidia tax, but only the largest players can afford to play.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Hyperscalers</span><span class=\"tag\">Custom silicon</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"LLMs Are Not Autocomplete — They Are Our Cognitive Cousins LLMs are not fancy autocomplete but ‘cognitive cousins’ that share core principles with human cognition: attention mechanisms recreate production systems, and prediction is the dominant training signal; sample efficiency is three to four orders of magnitude worse, but an architectural change may close the gap. Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape Mindscape 363 | Chandra Sripada on How LLMs and Humans are Cognitive Cousins Cognitive science Cognitive cousins Production systems Dual-process theory Predictive coding LLM mechanisms production system in-weight processing in-context processing contravariance architectural canalization chain of thought\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-mindscape-预测是训练信号之母-llm-是认知表亲/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://static.libsyn.com/p/assets/b/0/6/d/b06d098337897af116c3140a3186d450/21d641ef836c48dff270cbede4360d187bdd6c56cb126be7d6f51078db34d4675527fa72e13c86bb573ec42c5d4fc493e18e7d95fec734ec77168fc379baefcf.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">1:51:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>LLMs Are Not Autocomplete — They Are Our Cognitive Cousins</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">LLMs are not fancy autocomplete but ‘cognitive cousins’ that share core principles with human cognition: attention mechanisms recreate production systems, and prediction is the dominant training signal; sample efficiency is three to four orders of magnitude worse, but an architectural change may close the gap.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cognitive science</span><span class=\"tag\">Cognitive cousins</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Death Is the Last Enemy, Not the Final Word: A Former Senator on Borrowed Time Ben Sasse, a former senator with late-stage pancreatic cancer, gives this interview on borrowed time: his tumor has shrunk by more than 80%, but the disease may not be curable. He calls death ‘the last enemy, but not the final word’, and traces executive overreach back to legislative retreat. EconTalk EconTalk Ben Sasse on Faith, Family, and Facing Death Pancreatic cancer Facing death University governance Executive power Faith Digital revolution Heidelberg Catechism shared governance accelerationism safetyism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-econtalk-把死亡视为最后的敌人而非最终话语/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://image.simplecastcdn.com/images/4ca709a1-1918-43a4-9035-1176b5aa9f2b/b8c673f8-58e7-4d4a-ab02-f83c2fd463c4/3000x3000/econtalknewbluecover1400.jpg?aid=rss_feed\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:21:11</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Death Is the Last Enemy, Not the Final Word: A Former Senator on Borrowed Time</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ben Sasse, a former senator with late-stage pancreatic cancer, gives this interview on borrowed time: his tumor has shrunk by more than 80%, but the disease may not be curable. He calls death ‘the last enemy, but not the final word’, and traces executive overreach back to legislative retreat.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Pancreatic cancer</span><span class=\"tag\">Facing death</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Postliterate: Everyone Is Reading, and No One Reads at Length Anymore The share of Americans who read a book or an article on a given day has fallen 40% in twenty years; the thing to watch is not a literacy crisis but postliteracy — abstract thought, schooling and political communication are exiting alongside long-form reading. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area We’re postliterate Postliteracy Attention economy Education Political communication Media theory Walter Ong postliterate secondary orality syllogism epithet\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-grayarea-后文字时代-人人都在读-却没人再读长文/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/d5e1ef20-e112-11e8-83b5-ff093e8a88a8/image/TileArtwork_TheGrayArea_3000x3000__2_.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">51:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Postliterate: Everyone Is Reading, and No One Reads at Length Anymore</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The share of Americans who read a book or an article on a given day has fallen 40% in twenty years; the thing to watch is not a literacy crisis but postliteracy — abstract thought, schooling and political communication are exiting alongside long-form reading.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Postliteracy</span><span class=\"tag\">Attention economy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"ESPN&#x27;s Four Years of Profit Bought Pixar, Marvel and Lucasfilm — and Then Held Disney Back Disney&#x27;s thirty-year empire was fed by two cash cows: VHS tapes and ESPN subscriber fees. Streaming dissolved the structural advantage — the IP is immortal, but the old business model no longer exists. Acquired Acquired Disney: The Renaissance and the Empire Cash Cows IP Assets Streaming Sports Rights Corporate Governance M&amp;A Strategy CAPS (Computer Animation Production System) story reels film rentals multiplane camera\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-acquired-迪士尼帝国由-espn-现金牛买下-也被它拖住/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://img.transistorcdn.com/GwI1v26-YH_dXUXm6cxbMAU8_kLZ0TucnptVEAbOElY/rs:fill:0:0:1/w:1400/h:1400/q:60/mb:500000/aHR0cHM6Ly9pbWct/dXBsb2FkLXByb2R1/Y3Rpb24udHJhbnNp/c3Rvci5mbS80ZmU3/ZGE4MTQ3NjAzZGZj/MWU5YzVjYzViNTAw/OTIwYS5qcGc.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">4:32:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Acquired</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>ESPN&#x27;s Four Years of Profit Bought Pixar, Marvel and Lucasfilm — and Then Held Disney Back</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Disney&#x27;s thirty-year empire was fed by two cash cows: VHS tapes and ESPN subscriber fees. Streaming dissolved the structural advantage — the IP is immortal, but the old business model no longer exists.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cash Cows</span><span class=\"tag\">IP Assets</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Rammed by Her Own Fleet: The 1%-Cost Torpedo Boat That Created the Destroyer In 1908 a 380-ton destroyer was rammed and sunk by a 10,000-ton cruiser during an exercise, killing 35 men. Behind the tragedy sits the torpedo boat, which overturned naval warfare at about 1% of a battleship&#x27;s cost and forced the destroyer into existence. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Naval Revolution: Triumph and Disaster Naval history Destroyers Torpedo boats Asymmetric warfare Wreck archaeology Unmanned systems torpedo boat destroyer 30-knotters ram bow engine telegraph rebreather Normand boiler\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-10-historyhit-鱼雷艇用1-的战列舰成本-逼出了驱逐舰兵种/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f67d4b795a40fffd3b2c/1786107596786-b919132c-165c-4d85-a7b9-1f6d3bfc86bd.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">36:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0810</span></div>\n<h2>Rammed by Her Own Fleet: The 1%-Cost Torpedo Boat That Created the Destroyer</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In 1908 a 380-ton destroyer was rammed and sunk by a 10,000-ton cruiser during an exercise, killing 35 men. Behind the tragedy sits the torpedo boat, which overturned naval warfare at about 1% of a battleship&#x27;s cost and forced the destroyer into existence.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Naval history</span><span class=\"tag\">Destroyers</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Philip Sidney died of a leg wound and became famous at his funeral The Anglo-Spanish cold war turns hot, and Philip Sidney finally gets his chance at glory on the battlefields of the Low Countries — only to die of a leg wound that need not have killed him. What made him a hero of Protestant Europe was the funeral, not the fighting. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 695. Elizabeth I vs The Catholics: The Shadow War (Part 5) Elizabeth I Anglo-Spanish War Philip Sidney Dutch Revolt Economic warfare 16th century cautionary towns fireships / hell-burners Politiques War of the Three Henrys\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-09-restishistory-philip-sidney-之死-英雄是被葬礼制造出来的/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/00c0a118-2426-11ee-b258-73d331d0123b/image/db3295fcd9d3a7113e8000e931f0541d.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0809</span></div>\n<h2>Philip Sidney died of a leg wound and became famous at his funeral</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Anglo-Spanish cold war turns hot, and Philip Sidney finally gets his chance at glory on the battlefields of the Low Countries — only to die of a leg wound that need not have killed him. What made him a hero of Protestant Europe was the funeral, not the fighting.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Elizabeth I</span><span class=\"tag\">Anglo-Spanish War</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Subsea Fiber Is Becoming the World&#x27;s Largest Listening Network: Not Spycraft, Physics Distributed acoustic sensing (DAS) turns ordinary fiber-optic cable into a continuous vibration monitor. Oil and gas well surveys, earthquake monitoring, whale tracking and anti-submarine ship detection all run on the same technology chain, and the ocean is rapidly losing its privacy. Asianometry Asianometry The Dark Fiber Listening Network Dark Fiber Distributed Acoustic Sensing Ocean Surveillance Earthquake Monitoring Maritime Security DAS (Distributed Acoustic Sensing) Dark Fiber OTDR (Optical Time Domain Reflectometry) VSP (Vertical Seismic Profiling) Geophone Rayleigh Scattering\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-09-asianometry-暗光纤正在把海底变成全球最大窃听网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i1.ytimg.com/vi/8939X_kFwG4/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">20:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Asianometry</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0809</span></div>\n<h2>Subsea Fiber Is Becoming the World&#x27;s Largest Listening Network: Not Spycraft, Physics</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Distributed acoustic sensing (DAS) turns ordinary fiber-optic cable into a continuous vibration monitor. Oil and gas well surveys, earthquake monitoring, whale tracking and anti-submarine ship detection all run on the same technology chain, and the ocean is rapidly losing its privacy.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Dark Fiber</span><span class=\"tag\">Distributed Acoustic Sensing</span>\n</div></div></a>"]